Hypervisor I used: VMware Workstation

## First step where I got stuck
Step number: Part B, Step 5
What the step says: "In Customize Hardware, choose NAT networking and a conservative RAM/CPU allocation that leaves enough memory for the host. For comparison, an older lab02-win11 VM used 4 GB RAM and 2 virtual processors; these values have not been verified for the new Ticket1-Windows11 guest. Capture the new guest's actual hardware settings before claiming them."
What I did: Attempted to set the CPU and Memory values for the new Windows 11 virtual machine in the hardware wizard.
What happened / what I had to guess: The guide did not specify exact numerical values for the new Ticket1-Windows11 VM (RAM in MB/GB and number of vCPUs), stating only that they were unverified. As someone who has never configured a hypervisor, I did not know what "conservative allocation" meant relative to the host, and I had to guess whether to assign 4 GB or 8 GB of RAM and 2 or 4 cores to satisfy Windows 11 system requirements without starving the host.

## Other places I hesitated
- Part A, Step 5 vs Part B, Step 5: Ubuntu was assigned 6 GB of RAM while Windows 11 was suggested at 4 GB. Usually Windows 11 requires more system memory than a minimal headless Ubuntu Server. On a 16 GB host, running both simultaneously with these numbers leaves only 6 GB for Windows host processes.
- Part C, Step 4: The step mentions enabling File and Printer Sharing (Echo Request - ICMPv4-In) in wf.msc. While the warning not to disable the whole firewall is excellent, a beginner might struggle to locate the exact rule among hundreds in Windows Firewall without an explicit filter or PowerShell command.

## Settings I would not have chosen without the document
- Splitting the virtual disk into multiple files for Windows 11 (64 GB) to make it easier to transfer across filesystems without hitting file-size limits.
- Enabling the specific ICMPv4-In firewall rule rather than turning off the entire Windows Defender Firewall when testing guest-to-guest ping.
- Selecting NAT networking explicitly to ensure both guests reside on the same isolated virtual subnet while retaining outbound internet access through the host.

## One thing that worked well
- The distinction in Part D, Step 3 between VM snapshots (short-term state rollbacks) and true backups is clearly stated and directly reinforces the Week 5 Storage & I/O 3-2-1 backup principle.
